            "composition": [
              {
                "id": "new:b1",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "markup": "Museum interpretations can change because new evidence appears, existing objects are studied with new methods, and historians ask different questions than earlier generations did. A label written fifty years ago may have reflected the best knowledge available then, yet later research can reveal new dates, makers, uses, or cultural meanings.",
                "ranges": [],
                "textStyle": null,
                "margins": null
              },
              {
                "id": "new:b2",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "markup": "Interpretations also change because museums increasingly examine who produced earlier accounts and whose perspectives were left out. Revising an interpretation does not necessarily mean the earlier one was careless; it can mean the museum is widening the frame, comparing more sources, and recognizing that evidence can support a richer and more complex account than before.",
                "ranges": [],
                "textStyle": null,
                "margins": null
              },
              {
                "id": "new:b3",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "markup": "They remain accountable to evidence by showing how conclusions are reached: grounding claims in objects, records, provenance, scientific analysis, and credible testimony. Responsible change is therefore not arbitrary revision but a transparent process in which interpretations are updated, qualified, and sometimes corrected in light of the strongest available evidence.",
                "ranges": [],
                "textStyle": null,
                "margins": null
              }
            ],
            "anchors": [],
