              {
                "id": "new:b1",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "markup": "Museum interpretations can change because new evidence appears, old evidence is reexamined, and curators develop better questions. A label that once seemed complete may look partial when archives, conservation, or archaeology add context.",
                "ranges": [],
                "textStyle": null,
                "margins": null
              },
              {
                "id": "new:b2",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "markup": "That change does not mean anything goes. Responsible interpretation stays tied to objects, documents, provenance, and the limits of what can be supported. When claims shift, the reasons for the shift should be traceable.",
                "ranges": [],
                "textStyle": null,
                "margins": null
              },
              {
                "id": "new:b3",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "markup": "So museums remain accountable by showing how conclusions were reached, what evidence was used, and where uncertainty remains. In that way, interpretation can evolve while still being answerable to the record.",
                "ranges": [],
                "textStyle": null,
                "margins": null
              }
